BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Let's Go! Bloomington
DTSTART:20180814T223000Z
DTEND:20180815T033000Z
DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:2o60qdjdqkef70a1ik0l67ek2q@google.com
CREATED:20180805T155326Z
DESCRIPTION:<br><b>Poetry / Performance / Discussion</b><b><br><b><br></b>L
 iasons&nbsp\;</b><br>In the Name of the People" is the first book by LIAISO
 NS\, a collective of authors from America\, France\, Italy\, Japan\, Korea\
 , Lebanon\, Mexico\, Quebec\, Russia\, Spain\, and Ukraine. The book is an 
 analysis and reflection on the global populist surge\, written from our exp
 eriences within the localities we inhabit. The upheaval and polarizations c
 aused by populist movements around the world indicates above all the urgenc
 y to develop global revolutionary perspectives\, and to make the necessary<
 br>connections to understand and act in the present.<br><br><a href="http:/
 /www.commonnotions.org/liaisons">http://www.commonnotions.org/liaisons</a><
 br><br><br><b>Cody St. Clair</b><br>A Ph.D. candidate in the English Depart
 ment at Indiana University. Their dissertation\, titled “Homeless Modernism
 s: Down-and-Out Politics in Early-Twentieth-Century U.S. Literature and Cul
 ture\,” maps the politicization of homelessness in 1930s U.S. fiction\, poe
 try\, and visual art. More broadly\, their research concerns histories of l
 abor\, protest\, and civil disobedience with an emphasis on radical-leftist
  and queer social movements. Their most recent article\, titled “A Dilettan
 te Unto Death: Richard Bruce Nugent’s Dilettante Aesthetic and Unambitious 
 Failure\,” was published by African American Review in 2017\, and they are 
 currently completing an article on the Cuban-American performance artist An
 a Mendieta.<br><a href="http://instagram.com/itchy_glowbo_blow">http://inst
 agram.com/itchy_glowbo_blow</a><br><br><b>Janan Alexandra</b><br>A Lebanese
 -American poet and first-year MFA candidate at Indiana University. She was 
 born in Nicosia\, Cyprus and now lives in Bloomington by way of Baltimore\,
  Los Angeles\, and Portland\, Maine. Her writing is informed by a determine
 d belief in the radical and liberatory work of paying attention—through lan
 guage &amp\; rigorous imagination. Janan is interested in the ways that our
  linguistic choices are overlaid with geography\, ecology\, trauma\, legaci
 es of colonialism\, war and exile. For the last three years she has taught 
 poetry and creative writing to youth\, and before that she helped to run th
 e Smith College Poetry Center. She has a BA in African-American Studies and
  Poetry from Smith College and has published poems in the Adroit Journal an
 d Rusted Radishes\, a literary journal coming out of Beirut\, Lebanon. Jana
 n believes in the truth-telling power of poems and is keen to disrupt linea
 r narratives\, create new language\, and make art under capitalism.&nbsp\;<
 br><a href="http://instagram.com/laptitevaliserouge">http://instagram.com/l
 aptitevaliserouge</a><br><br>6:30 $?<br><a href="https://www.facebook.com/e
 vents/2146297855642656/">FB EVENT</a>
LAST-MODIFIED:20180805T155326Z
LOCATION:Hopscotch Coffee Roastery & To Go
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Poetry & Spoken Word: Liasons / Cody St. Clair / Jana Alexandra
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
