UID:1vn7vvc1nlk26rce5eo0io8i20@google.com
CREATED:20180430T141434Z
DESCRIPTION:<br><strong>From FB (with edits):</strong><br><br><strong>Jaspe
 r Bernes</strong> (essay) (Oakland\, CA)<br>Jasper will present "Belly of t
 he Revolution\," a forthcoming essay on agriculture\, energy\, and the futu
 re of communism. Jasper is author of a scholarly book\, The Work of Art in 
 the Age of Deindustrialization (Stanford\, 2017)\, and two books of poetry\
 , Starsdown (2007) and We Are Nothing and So Can You (2015).  Essays\, poem
 s and other writings can be found in Critical Inquiry\, Modern Language Qua
 rterly\, Radical Philosophy\, Endnotes\, Lana Turner\, The American Reader\
 , and elsewhere. Together with Juliana Spahr and Joshua Clover\, he edits C
 ommune Editions.<br><br><strong>Adrian Matejka</strong> (poetry) (local)&nb
 sp\;<br>&nbsp\; Adrian was born in Nuremberg\, Germany and grew up in Calif
 ornia and Indiana. His first collection of poems\, The Devil’s Garden (2003
 )\, won the 2002 New York / New England Award. His second collection\, Mixo
 logy (2009)\, was a winner of the National Poetry Series. Mixology was a fi
 nalist for a NAACP Image Award for Outstanding Literature. The Big Smoke (2
 013)\, which focuses on the life of the boxer Jack Johnson\, was awarded th
 e 2014 Anisfield-Wolf Book Award and was a finalist for the National Book A
 ward and Pulitzer Prize. His most recent book is Map to the Stars (2017). A
 drian is the recipient of fellowships the Guggenheim Foundation and the Lan
 nan Foundation. He teaches creative writing at Indiana University in Bloomi
 ngton.<br><br><a href="https://www.facebook.com/JamesDavidPayne"><font colo
 r="#000080"><strong>James Payne</strong></font></a> (poetry) (Columbus\, OH
 )<br>James is a writer who teaches in the History of Art and visual Culture
  department at the Columbus College of Art &amp\; Design in Columbus\, Ohio
 . Monster House Press publishedd his poetry collection\, Things Just Aren't
  They\, in 2015.<br><br><strong>Theo Cornetta</strong> (fiber) (Indy)<br><b
 r><br><a href="https://www.facebook.com/benlums"><font color="#000080"><str
 ong>Ben Lumsdaine</strong></font></a> (drums) (local)<br><br><br>Also\, we'
 ll have 50-100 baby plants in peat pots from Hart Farm for attendees to tak
 e home. All the little seedlings will grow into food\, greens\, tomatoes or
  herbs\, whether you plant it in the ground directly or kept in peat pot to
  grow. If you have seedlings or seeds to give away or swap\, bring 'em ~<br
 ><br><strong>Beneficiaries of the door:</strong><br>Bloomington Community O
 rchard <br><a href="http://l.facebook.com/l.php?u=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.blooming
 toncommunityorchard.org%2F&amp\;h=ATN1kC8mgihB1aXllBZVOU1vVLlwPYBGT-6g98Kx1
 GOhJYcMA6Mzvw1UXt595p8dOJVcSA1GLXIo2OdFUyqrZIBSW0GmknwLr-XkY70RvsNnrb-x&amp
 \;s=1"><font color="#000080">http://www.bloomingtoncommunityorchard.org</fo
 nt></a><br><br><br>Kheprw Institute's Growing Good In the Hood<br><a href="
 http://l.facebook.com/l.php?u=http%3A%2F%2Fkheprw.org%2Fgrowin-good-in-the-
 hood%2F&amp\;h=ATOsFxsj6phHOX_5jTMGiiZ3AvXUYOd61LOpyBQbq145C0CplK0QkG8EKRx5
 hCgD_ZFmkENgN0WwymXKiFzO9cbch6z1lChfa66vUX4lMKWy_Yxa&amp\;s=1"><font color=
 "#000080">http://kheprw.org/growin-good-in-the-hood/</font></a><br><br>MHPr
 esents is supported in part by the City of Bloomington Arts Commission's Ar
 ts Project Grant Program &amp\; the BUEA Zone Arts Grant.<br><br>7:pm ALL A
 GES.<br><a href="https://www.facebook.com/events/187093155145821/">FB EVENT
 </a>
LAST-MODIFIED:20180430T141434Z
LOCATION:I Fell Building
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Monster House Press Presents: Belly of the Revolution
TRANSP:OPAQUE
