BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Let's Go! Bloomington
DTSTART:20090226T010000Z
DTEND:20090226T040000Z
DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:45hbuj2m4gk6oqsi9jgpncp3a4@google.com
CREATED:20090105T203007Z
DESCRIPTION:Spirit of '68\, The BCT\, and ArtsWeek present...\n<br><br>\nDE
 AD PREZ<br>\nWednesday February 25th<Br>\n@ The Buskirk-Chumley Theater<Br>
 \n8pm<Br>\n$15<br>\nTickets available at Landlocked Music\, Tracks\, the Su
 nrise Box Office\, and bloomingtonarts.info\n<br><br>\nDead Prez<Br>\n-----
 ---------\nFor more than a decade\, Dead Prez have set the standard in unde
 rground hip hop for radical idealism\, political action\, "common sense" po
 litical analysis and leading a healthy and positive lifestyle. They are lar
 gely known for their explosive live performances\, hard-hitting style and p
 olitically astute lyrics focusing on racism\, critical pedagogy\, religion\
 , activism against governmental repression\, and corporate control over the
  media\, especially hip-hop record labels.\n<Br><Br>\nThe group has held a 
 strong following since well before their 2000 debut album\, Let's Get Free\
 , was released on Loud records.\n<br><Br>\nDead Prez's songs weave messages
  of black power\, radical political thought\, and grass roots values with p
 owerful\, soul-felt music. On stage\, they have been known to criticize mon
 ey-grubbing corporations with flaming dollar bills\, and encourage the audi
 ence to “live healthy” by handing out crates of apples. Their music spans a
  tremendous range of timely sociopolitical issues\, all the more remarkable
  in wake of the current popularity of monotonous faux-gangsta' declarations
  and sex-raps.\n<br><br>\nDead Prez are a torrential force of hip-hop music
  and revolutionary thought. 
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T050838Z
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Dead Prez
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
