BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Let's Go! Bloomington
DTSTART:20080915T000000Z
DTEND:20080915T030000Z
DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:befgik8jphrb9sv7pehdv0coo0@google.com
CREATED:20080912T140127Z
DESCRIPTION:Spirit of '68 Promotions proudly presents:\n<br><br>\nLANGHORNE
  SLIM\nTHE BUILDERS AND THE BUTCHERS\n@ The Bluebird\n8pm\n$8\n<br><br>\nLa
 nghorne Slim\nwww.myspace.com/langhorneslim\nSomething of a one-man mixture
  of the Cramps\, Beck's early indie records\n(circa One Foot in the Grave)\
 , and the soundtrack to O Brother Where Art\nThou\, singer and guitarist La
 nghorne Slim offers a sardonic\, modern take on\ntraditional folk\, country
 \, and blues. Fancifully dubbed "the bastard son\nofHasil Adkins" in some o
 f his early press releases\, Langhorne Slim is in\nfact a Pennsylvania nati
 ve who resettled in Brooklyn after his graduation\nfrom the State Universit
 y of New York at Purchase. After a self-released\ndemo garnered some local 
 and online attention (as well as a semi-regular gig\nas the opening act for
  indie novelty outfit the Trachtenberg Family\nSlideshow Players)\, Langhor
 ne Slim signed with the indie label Narnack\nRecords and released his first
  EP\, Electric Love Letter\, in March 2004. The\nmore varied and band-orien
 ted full-length When the Sun's Gone Down followed\nin the spring of 2005. M
 uch touring ensued over the next year\, including\nsupport dates with Lucer
 o and Murder by Death\, with drummer Malachi\nDeLorenzo and upright bassist
  Paul DeFiglia (aka "the War Eagles") in tow.\nIn 2006\, Langhorne Slim sig
 ned with the larger (though still not major)\nlabel V2 Records\, which rele
 ased the all-new EPEngine in September of that\nyear\, as the singer was fi
 nishing recording his second full album\, produced\nby Josh Ritter's keyboa
 rdist\, Sam Kassirer. The deal fell through\, however\,\nand the band was l
 eft labelless. Langhorne Slim found a new home on Kemado\nRecords\, who rel
 eased the self-titled album in the spring of 2008.\n<br><br>\nThe Builders 
 and the Butchers\nwww.myspace.com/thebuildersandthebutchers\nAlthough the B
 uilders and the Butchers formed in Portland in the mid-2000s\,\nthe band's 
 hybrid of folk\, country\, and twangy roots rock evokes the Great\nDepressi
 on more than the contemporary Northwest. The group's beginnings date\nback 
 to fall 2003\, when frontman Ryan Sollee relocated from Alaska to\nPortland
  with his then-current band\, the Born Losers. A newfound fascination\nwith
  blues\, country\, and gospel music convinced Sollee to launch a different\
 nproject\, and the musician teamed up with several local musicians --\nincl
 uding bassist Alex Ellis\, organist/percussionist Ray Rude\, drummer Paul\n
 Seely\, and mandolin/banjo player Harvey Tumbleson -- to form the Builders\
 nand the Butchers. Playing their first show in fall 2005 (on Halloween\nwee
 kend\, no less)\, the Builders quickly found an audience with their dark\,\
 nSouthern gothic songcraft\, most of which was played on acoustic instrumen
 ts\nwith little amplification. Although the band was forced to add amplifie
 rs as\nthe crowds increased\, the down-home appeal still remained\, and loc
 al\nAmericana label Bladen County Records soon signed the Builders and the\
 nButchers to their roster. A self-titled LP followed in March 2007.\n<br><b
 r>\nTickets available at Landlocked Music and ticketmaster.com\n<br><br>\nF
 or more information\, check out http://www.spiritof68promotions.com
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T050733Z
LOCATION:The Bluebird
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Langhorne Slim\, The Builders & The Butchers
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
