UID:62krn3gkde2dnvugef7v62ikak@google.com
CREATED:20090929T185540Z
DESCRIPTION:The School of Fine Arts (SoFA) Gallery at Indiana University is
  proud to present the lecture “Art\, the Anthropocene\, and Global Change” 
 by renowned writer William L. Fox on Friday\, October 2 at 5:30 in Woodburn
  Hall 101. There will be a reception in the SoFA Gallery following the lect
 ure. This lecture is in conjunction with the exhibition The Canary Project:
  Works on Climate Change 2006-2009. All events are free and open to the pub
 lic.\n\nWilliam L Fox’s lecture “Art\, the Anthropocene\, and Global Change
 ” will address The Art of the Anthropocene\, or Why Environmental Art? He w
 ill trace three concurrent historical trends that led to the Canary Project
  and other artistic ventures dealing with global change: the three stages o
 f the Anthropocene\, the three great eras of exploration\, and the three pr
 imary artistic technologies for representing our relationship to landscape.
 Fox will show examples from Alexander von Humboldt and Frederic Church thro
 ugh the work of Helen and Newton Harrison\, and the Land Arts of the Americ
 an West program.\n\nWilliam L. Fox is a writer whose work is a sustained in
 quiry into how human cognition transforms land into landscape. His numerous
  nonfiction books rely upon fieldwork with artists and scientists in extrem
 e environments to provide the narratives through which he conducts his inve
 stigations. He also serves as the Director of the Center for Art + Environm
 ent at the Nevada Museum of Art in Reno.\n\nEdward Morris\, co-founder of t
 he Canary Project\, speaks to the importance of Fox’s lecture to the Canary
  Project: “Bill does a better job than anyone I know or read of exploring h
 uman efforts to understand and control our environment. He is unique in his
  ability to traverse the languages of science and art\, a challenge at the 
 core of The Canary Project. He is also a fantastic speaker. We believe his 
 recent focus on the concept of the Anthropocene (a term for describing our 
 current geological epoch as one in which the influence of man has become do
 minant) is extremely important for coming to terms with the risks inherent 
 in pursuing our current mode of development.”\n\nRenowned as innovators in 
 the field of interdisciplinary and activist art\, The Canary Project produc
 es visual media and artworks about the single issue of climate change. This
  exhibit brings together works by more than a dozen Canary Project artists\
 , including project co-founders Susannah Sayler and Edward Morris. Collecti
 vely these works explore ways in which the prospect of catastrophic climate
  change challenges the limits of human comprehension and agency. The exhibi
 tion posits that these challenges can be a source of inspiration\, melancho
 lic beauty\, engagement or defeat.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T050721Z
LOCATION:Woodburn Hall 101
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Lecture: “Art\, the Anthropocene\, and Global Change”
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
