DESCRIPTION:Spirit of '68 Promotions and The Cinemat present:\n<br><br>\nGE
 NTLEMAN CALLER (CD release)<br>\nTHE COUNTRY DEATH (Bloomington)<br>\nTHE E
 ND OF THE WORLD (NYC)<br>\n@ The Cinemat\nFriday\, November 14\n9pm\n$3 (ye
 s\, you are reading this correctly\, only $3!)\nALL AGES (with beer availab
 le for sale to the aged)\n<br><br>\nGENTLEMAN CALLER\nhttp://www.myspace.co
 m/thegentlemancaller\nKenny Childers and his entourage of friends and music
 ians\, from such legendary Indiana bands as Brando\, The Mysteries of Life\
 , The Blake Babies\, Sardina\, etc.\, have recorded a new album! For one ni
 ght only\, they would like to share some new songs with you in a live perfo
 rmance at The Cinemat in Bloomington\, IN. The new record will be available
  for sale at this show.\n<br><br>\nThe Country Death (Bloomington)\nhttp://
 www.myspace.com/countrydeath\nBloomington's premiere country-rock 4-some wi
 ll return to the Cinemat's stage!\n<Br><br>\nThe End of the World (NYC\, Fl
 ameshovel Records)\nhttp://www.myspace.com/theendoftheworld\nThis rock grou
 p from NYC will making a stop in Bloomington while on tour in support of th
 eir new record on Flameshovel Records! The Onion says some kind words:\n*"S
 tefan Marolachakis delivers each line as though it were part of a speech th
 at's going to take a while to finish\; when he pauses\, you can almost hear
  him reaching for a glass of water. ...and when Marolachakis weaves his woo
 zy croon through the beat\, You're Making It Come Alive frequently lives up
  to its title. On songs like the hustling 'Party's Over\,' The End Of The W
 orld creates an electric racket that's similar to Pavement's early singles.
  It's like someone accidentally recorded an expectedly intense performance 
 in some basement in the middle of nowhere."* - *The Onion*\n\n<br><br>\nwww
 .spiritof68promotions.com 
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051435Z
LOCATION:The Cinemat
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Gentleman Caller (CD release show)\, The Country Death
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Let's Go! Bloomington
DTSTART:20091008T010000Z
DTEND:20091008T050000Z
DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:0ads9og7qp8nk9i748u8nv0vcg@google.com
CREATED:20090820T134039Z
DESCRIPTION:NURSES\nw/ husband&wife\n@ The Bishop\nWednesday October 7th\n9
 pm\n$7\n18+\n\nTo immerse yourself in Nurses' Apple's Acre is to delight in
  a certain mental unraveling. These are songs where the vulnerability of po
 p with its heart on its sleeve engages in a double-dutch jumprope match wit
 h the euphoric surrender-to-the-weird that is essential to psychedelia. And
  Nurses is a band that scavenges beauty and wonder\, uncovering Technicolor
  where others see somber hues. Their off-kilter psych-pop is driven by ever
 -swelling vocal harmonies\, adventurous electronics\, some serious wheelin'
  and dealin' on the Rhodes piano\, and the kind of new-primitivist percussi
 on that may or may not involve a standard drum kit.\n\nAn encyclopedic list
  of musical influences and sound-alikes won't necessarily cozy up your brai
 n to the Nurses experience. Their compositions revel in the unexpected\, an
 d it creates music that's endlessly fascinating. Part of the allure is that
  the songs of Aaron Chapman and John Bowers--the cosmic troubadours and lon
 gtime best friends behind Nurses--are in a constant state of motion\, as if
  they're evolving while you listen. Perhaps it's no surprise\, since Chapma
 n and Bowers themselves have spent the past five years in a state of transi
 ence\, as friendly nomads who gave up the comforts of normalcy for the deep
 er satisfaction of total devotion to making music. If the two aren't rambli
 ng in their own sonic imaginations\, they're not satisfied.\n\nLuckily for 
 Portland\, Ore.\, they settled down there for a while and unpacked their ex
 hilarating playfulness in the attic of a Victorian house. It was in their a
 ttic fort that they recorded much of Apple's Acre. Now that the pair call P
 ortland home\, they have expanded to a trio and are joined by percussionist
  and visual artist James Mitchell.\n\nApple's Acre is a record full of dysf
 unctional\, hushed love\, laced with elegance and grace and without angst a
 nd regret. But the plain courage of their songs is the secret ingredient th
 at's already turning heads and blowing minds all over the Northwest--and if
  you think about it\, in pop music\, courage is always at a premium.\n\nwww
 .myspace.com/nurses\nwww.myspace.com/husbandwife
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051547Z
LOCATION:The Bishop\,  123 S Walnut Street
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Nurses w/ husband&wife
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
