DTSTART:20090804T010000Z
DTEND:20090804T040000Z
DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:mjj5h25s22soag5l8av4828hj0@google.com
CREATED:20090721T133103Z
DESCRIPTION:Spirit of '68 Promotions Presents:\n\nWOODEN WAND\n(a.k.a. Wand
 \, a.k.a. James Jackson Toth)\nNORMANOAK\n(a.k.a. Chris Barth\, of the Impo
 ssible Shapes)\n@ Russian Recording\n1021 S. Walnut St.\nMonday\, August 3r
 d\n9pm. (for real)\n$5.\nALL AGES\n\n\nWOODEN WAND\nhttp://www.myspace.com/
 woodenwand\nhttp://www.jamesjacksontoth.com/media\n\nIn the plethora of mod
 ern psychedlic-folk music (let alone all popular music)\, it is difficult t
 o find a lyricist/songwriter more prolific than James Jackson Toth. In the 
 course of his musical career\, he has released countless records (all worth
 y of your ears)\, under countless monikers (including Wand\, Wooden Wand\, 
 Wooden Wand and the Vanishing Voice\, Wooden Wand and the Sky High Band\, a
 mong others)\, through countless record labels (including Rykodisc\, 5RC\, 
 Ecstatic Peace\, Kill Rock Stars\, Troubleman Unlimited\, and soon: Young G
 od). Don't miss this chance to see JJT perform as Wooden Wand on August 3rd
  at Russian Recording.\n\nBelow are a few short descriptions of a few of JJ
 T's records:\n\nWAND - "Born Bad" (Mad Monk/ People In A Position To Know R
 ecords\, 2009):\nAfter the worst year of James Jackson Toth's life\; band q
 uitting mid-tour\, his long-term musical and life partner leaving\, a split
  with his label and his long-time manager\, he records one of the best albu
 ms of his (very prolific) career.\n\nWAND - "Hard Knox" collection of home 
 recordings from 2002-2007 (Ecstatic Peace Records\, 2009)\n"To quickly addr
 ess the elephant in the room - certainly\, collections of demos\, outtakes 
 and home recordings are mostly bogus\, but obviously you're reading this\, 
 so obviously I've somehow been coerced into releasing this batch of tunes\,
  and you've bought it or stolen it or borrowed it or gotten a promo or what
 ever\, so let's cut to the chase. In my defense\, all of the cuts contained
  herein are 'songs' in the traditional western sense - my experiments in "s
 urf harmonica" and "doom zydeco" will not be chronicled here\, deep and ple
 ntiful as those archives may be. You've already pardoned the narcissism\, n
 ow pardon the cliche: I stand behind these songs as 'snapshots' and enjoy t
 hem despite their many flaws. I hope you do\, too." - JJT\n\nJAMES JACKSON 
 TOTH - "Waiting in Vain" (Rykodisc Records\, 2008)\nFor his solo debut albu
 m [that is\, under his own name]\, the Tennessee-based singer and songwrite
 r James Jackson Toth has conjured a mysterious\, alluring world peopled by 
 characters that could have been pulled from the pages of a Faulkner novel o
 r from one of photographer William Eggleston's stark portraits of Memphis b
 arflies. Toth's scenarios are intriguingly tawdry\, his sounds tantalizing\
 , with beautifully layered\, almost-dreamlike harmonies\; touches of blues\
 , country and soul\; the occasional flash of punk swagger\; and even some s
 weet Fleetwood Mac-inspired pop. With his evocative\, world-weary drawl of 
 a voice\, Toth assumes the role of storyteller\, maybe even confessor\, spi
 nning inter-linked tales of hope and misfortune\, romantic trials and spiri
 tual yearning. Toth has impressive\, vinyl-era ambition\; he's fashioned a 
 disc that works as a cohesive whole\, sequenced with such old-school care i
 t could practically be called a concept album.\n\nWAND - "James and the Qui
 et" (Ecstatic Peace Records\, 2007)\nWooden Wand taps into the dark\, crypt
 ic\, desolate-cornfield aspect of American music.\n\nWOODEN WAND and the VA
 NISHING VOICE - "Gypsy Freedom" (5RC Records\, 2006)\nWhile past releases s
 aw the band's avant folk dosed somewhat conservatively with the blues\, fre
 e jazz\, heavy psych\, and metal\, Gipsy Freedom brings these other\, non-f
 olk elements to the fore and generally turns the volume up. It's the band's
  most confounding and rewarding release. You may hear the faint echoes of I
 sland Harvest-era Albert Ayler on one track\, Iommi-derived riffage on the 
 next\, torch songs that sound torn straight from the Gershwin songbook on t
 he next\, and Can-style epic grooves on the next. Radically different\, yet
  sustaining the group's high level of musical and thematic consistency\, Gi
 psy Freedom is the sound of a band breaking out of the box and using the di
 scarded shapes to construct strange new universes.\n\n\nNORMANOAK\nhttp://w
 ww.myspace.com/normanoak\n\nChris Barth. "now plucking at an amplified Rubb
 er Band Tin Can musickal instrument and uttering forth . . ."\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051728Z
LOCATION:Russian Recording
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Wooden Wand
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
