UID:ti59h3ib1ut8qt5hmavp3ub7lk@google.com
CREATED:20081006T150923Z
DESCRIPTION:Theatre of the People presents Project I:\nHenrik Ibsen Revival
 \n<br><br>\n“A Dolls House” and “An Enemy of the People”\n<Br><Br>\n$12 Gen
 eral Admission\n$10 Students/Seniors\n<Br><br>\n**$8 Facebook Special**\nTe
 ll the Box Office Volunteer you heard about this show on Facebook and recie
 ve a $2-$4 discount. Special Facebook Discount applies at the door only!\n<
 Br><br>\nGeneral admission/Student/Senior tickets available at the door or 
 online at www.bloomingtonarts.info\n<Br><br>\n\nPRESS RELEASE:\n<br><br>\nB
 loomington\, Ind.\nTheatre of the People (TOP)\, a new nonprofit community 
 theatre based in Bloomington\, Indiana\, is proud to present Henrik Ibsen R
 evival: A Dolls House & An Enemy of the People. This double-one act feature
  of daring deeds offers audiences two plays for the price of one\, and the 
 cast and crew is comprised of IU students\, Bloomington residents\, and oth
 er Hoosiers.\n<Br><br>\n"For the price of one show\, Hoosiers will get to e
 njoy two time-honored stories presented in an unconventional and highly ent
 ertaining way. This double-feature of Ibsen classics gives audiences a uniq
 ue opportunity to glimpse how the Nouveau Realism and Dada movements have a
 ffected\, and are affecting\, performance art\," says A Dolls House directo
 r and IU Theatre & Drama alumna Hannah Moss.\n<Br><br>\n"Theatre as an art 
 form has experienced a shift in audience demographic for the past several d
 ecades in that audiences are growing older and getting sparse because young
 er audiences have grown up in a fast-paced and graphic-laden environment of
  instant gratification. TOP has modernized both century-old plays to hopefu
 lly connect these worthwhile and meaningful stories to all audiences in a f
 resh and provocative way."\n<Br><br>\nTOP has adapted both A Dolls House an
 d An Enemy of the People to an approximate one-hour length each to accommod
 ate a traditional two-hour performance\, and there were no revisions to Ibs
 en's original linguistic style or prose in either script except the subtle 
 injection of local and modern references. For example\, An Enemy of the Peo
 ple is set in Bloomington's "Peoples Park" near the IU Sample Gates on Kirk
 wood Avenue\, Downtown\, and the water for the town's Baths is pumped from 
 Lake Monroe. A Doll's House in set in a Barbie Dream Home\, complete with F
 rench maids\, Skipper\, and Ken\, to draw a provocative and tantalizing par
 allel between the lives of 19th-century elite and the phenomenon we now kno
 w as the 'celebutante'.\n<Br><br>\nTheatre of the People (TOP)\, a non-prof
 it community theatre based in Bloomington\, Indiana\, is committed to incre
 asing public awareness of relevant humanitarian issues by facilitating comm
 unity expression through the presentation of artistic experiences and other
  educational opportunities.\n<Br><br>\n"Because TOP's mission is aimed at i
 ncreasing public awareness of relevant humanitarian issues\, TOP has infuse
 d modern meaning through local perspectives into two of Ibsen's late 19th C
 entury masterpieces. TOP has kept Ibsen's originality intact\, but we've in
 corporated the humanitarian themes of gender roles and celebrity worship in
 to A Dolls House and gender identity and mass poverty into An Enemy of the 
 People\," says An Enemy of the People director and IU Continuing Studies st
 udent David Nosko.\n<br><br>\n"Audiences familiar with either Ibsen play wi
 ll hopefully enjoy the modern interpretations of very traditional shows in 
 that TOP has made every effort to showcase the best of Ibsen in a thought-p
 rovoking way. Audiences unfamiliar with either Ibsen play will hopefully fe
 el turned on by the sexiness that is an Ibsen-imagined world\, tune in to I
 bsen's beautiful use of language and story structure\, and drop out of them
 selves for a couple hours to immerse their mind in the deeper implications 
 of Ibsen's worldview."\n<Br><bR>\nTOP aims to present new works of theatric
 al art and to fold traditional tales of dramatic work inside out. TOP's ima
 ginative onstage storytelling incorporates collage and decollage principles
 \, and TOP's unique minimalist creativity is inspired by the avant-garde mo
 vements of Dada\, Nouveau Realism\, and Epic Theatre. For the People and By
  the People\, TOP does modern art for everyone's sake.\n<Br><br>\nTOP is co
 mprised of all volunteer actors and artists who form the TOP Artistic Colla
 borative Troupe (ACT). TOP ACT members for the "Henrik Ibsen Revival" inclu
 des 12 IU students: freshman Sean Cole\, sophomore Abby Cullen\, senior Ste
 phanie Dittus\, freshman Meg Duell\, junior Meg Dwyer\, sophomore Keetin Ma
 rchi\, freshman Nicholas Maudlin\, senior Claire Mitchell\, freshman Molly 
 Rose\, sophomore Alize Rozsnyai\, junior Eric Van Gucht\, sophomore Christi
 na Vanko\; Ivy Tech freshman Paige Talbert\, Bloomington High School South 
 seniors Erica Cunningham and Travis Lampke\, Bloomington resident Chris LeT
 endre and Bloomington youth Meggie Bontrager\, Sarah Leaffer\, and Owen Wal
 ters.\n<Br><br>\n"The cast has is a wonderful blend of seasoned actors and 
 relatively new actors\, but all of them have blended together to create an 
 ensemble that is strong in leadership\, personality\, and talent. I am very
  proud of the professional way in which they have approached this heavy pie
 ce\, and the actors have worked hard to realize these characters both as cl
 assical literary figures and as modern personalities with modern challenges
 \," Moss says.\n<br><br>\n"Balancing lighthearted anachronisms with the ser
 ious nature of the dramatic situations Ibsen's characters find themselves i
 n is a welcomed challenge of this project. I am proud of TOP for taking on 
 two well-known and courageous plays and finding the freedom to explore them
  in ways that this community has never seen before."\n<Br><Br>\nQUOTES FROM
  A DOLLS HOUSE CAST:\n"It is very exciting to work on a piece that is still
  socially relevant today\," says IU freshman Meg Duell\, who portrays Nora.
  "Although women have come a long way since Ibsen's time\, the play's theme
 s and messages translate to a new audience that face different obstacles."\
 n<Br><Br>\n"There is a continuing theme of manipulation throughout the show
  that provides political commentary on how citizens in 21st Century view ea
 ch other: as playthings\," says freshman Nicholas Maudlin\, who portrays To
 rvald. "I hope audiences can enjoy the subtleties behind the dialogue and e
 njoy the humor of the show as a whole."\n<Br><Br>\n"I hope audiences realiz
 e that the characters portrayed on stage are more than they seem because no
 body is universally villainous or universally heroic\," says IU junior Eric
  Van Gucht\, who portrays Dr. Rank. "I hope audiences leave the show questi
 oning the characters' motives but also realizing how relevant Ibsen's argum
 ents are today."\n<Br><Br>\nQUOTES FROM AN ENEMY OF THE PEOPLE CAST:\n"I ho
 pe audiences will accept our new and interpretation of the script. I think 
 the absurdist point of view we are taking is rather enlightening in a way t
 hat means we can take risks and have the audience will learn from those ris
 ks\," said IU freshman and Assistant Director Sean Cole. "Think of it as an
  out of body experience—just sit back and enjoy what you are experiencing."
 \n<Br><Br>\n"This is NOT classic Ibsen. I am NOT the classical Thomas Stock
 mann. We are NOT your average production team\," says Bloomington High Scho
 ol South senior Erica Cunningham\, who portrays Dr. Stockmann. "I hope audi
 ences can experience a new spin on classic theatre and a fresh new kind of 
 theatre all together."
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051058Z
LOCATION:Waldron Arts Center
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Project I: The Henrik Ibsen Revival
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
