DTSTAMP:20260905T041548Z
UID:gbj2lmgb06o8obo0jojhftnmuo@google.com
CREATED:20081006T151009Z
DESCRIPTION:Theatre of the People presents Project I:\nHenrik Ibsen Revival
 \n\n\n\n“A Dolls House” and “An Enemy of the People”\n\n\n\n$12 General Adm
 ission\n$10 Students/Seniors\n\n\n\n**$8 Facebook Special**\nTell the Box O
 ffice Volunteer you heard about this show on Facebook and recieve a $2-$4 d
 iscount. Special Facebook Discount applies at the door only!\n\n\n\nGeneral
  admission/Student/Senior tickets available at the door or online at www.bl
 oomingtonarts.info\n\n\n\n\nPRESS RELEASE:\n\n\n\nBloomington\, Ind.\nTheat
 re of the People (TOP)\, a new nonprofit community theatre based in Bloomin
 gton\, Indiana\, is proud to present Henrik Ibsen Revival: A Dolls House & 
 An Enemy of the People. This double-one act feature of daring deeds offers 
 audiences two plays for the price of one\, and the cast and crew is compris
 ed of IU students\, Bloomington residents\, and other Hoosiers.\n\n\n\n"For
  the price of one show\, Hoosiers will get to enjoy two time-honored storie
 s presented in an unconventional and highly entertaining way. This double-f
 eature of Ibsen classics gives audiences a unique opportunity to glimpse ho
 w the Nouveau Realism and Dada movements have affected\, and are affecting\
 , performance art\," says A Dolls House director and IU Theatre & Drama alu
 mna Hannah Moss.\n\n\n\n"Theatre as an art form has experienced a shift in 
 audience demographic for the past several decades in that audiences are gro
 wing older and getting sparse because younger audiences have grown up in a 
 fast-paced and graphic-laden environment of instant gratification. TOP has 
 modernized both century-old plays to hopefully connect these worthwhile and
  meaningful stories to all audiences in a fresh and provocative way."\n\n\n
 \nTOP has adapted both A Dolls House and An Enemy of the People to an appro
 ximate one-hour length each to accommodate a traditional two-hour performan
 ce\, and there were no revisions to Ibsen's original linguistic style or pr
 ose in either script except the subtle injection of local and modern refere
 nces. For example\, An Enemy of the People is set in Bloomington's "Peoples
  Park" near the IU Sample Gates on Kirkwood Avenue\, Downtown\, and the wat
 er for the town's Baths is pumped from Lake Monroe. A Doll's House in set i
 n a Barbie Dream Home\, complete with French maids\, Skipper\, and Ken\, to
  draw a provocative and tantalizing parallel between the lives of 19th-cent
 ury elite and the phenomenon we now know as the 'celebutante'.\n\n\n\nTheat
 re of the People (TOP)\, a non-profit community theatre based in Bloomingto
 n\, Indiana\, is committed to increasing public awareness of relevant human
 itarian issues by facilitating community expression through the presentatio
 n of artistic experiences and other educational opportunities.\n\n\n\n"Beca
 use TOP's mission is aimed at increasing public awareness of relevant human
 itarian issues\, TOP has infused modern meaning through local perspectives 
 into two of Ibsen's late 19th Century masterpieces. TOP has kept Ibsen's or
 iginality intact\, but we've incorporated the humanitarian themes of gender
  roles and celebrity worship into A Dolls House and gender identity and mas
 s poverty into An Enemy of the People\," says An Enemy of the People direct
 or and IU Continuing Studies student David Nosko.\n\n\n\n"Audiences familia
 r with either Ibsen play will hopefully enjoy the modern interpretations of
  very traditional shows in that TOP has made every effort to showcase the b
 est of Ibsen in a thought-provoking way. Audiences unfamiliar with either I
 bsen play will hopefully feel turned on by the sexiness that is an Ibsen-im
 agined world\, tune in to Ibsen's beautiful use of language and story struc
 ture\, and drop out of themselves for a couple hours to immerse their mind 
 in the deeper implications of Ibsen's worldview."\n\n\n\nTOP aims to presen
 t new works of theatrical art and to fold traditional tales of dramatic wor
 k inside out. TOP's imaginative onstage storytelling incorporates collage a
 nd decollage principles\, and TOP's unique minimalist creativity is inspire
 d by the avant-garde movements of Dada\, Nouveau Realism\, and Epic Theatre
 . For the People and By the People\, TOP does modern art for everyone's sak
 e.\n\n\n\nTOP is comprised of all volunteer actors and artists who form the
  TOP Artistic Collaborative Troupe (ACT). TOP ACT members for the "Henrik I
 bsen Revival" includes 12 IU students: freshman Sean Cole\, sophomore Abby 
 Cullen\, senior Stephanie Dittus\, freshman Meg Duell\, junior Meg Dwyer\, 
 sophomore Keetin Marchi\, freshman Nicholas Maudlin\, senior Claire Mitchel
 l\, freshman Molly Rose\, sophomore Alize Rozsnyai\, junior Eric Van Gucht\
 , sophomore Christina Vanko\; Ivy Tech freshman Paige Talbert\, Bloomington
  High School South seniors Erica Cunningham and Travis Lampke\, Bloomington
  resident Chris LeTendre and Bloomington youth Meggie Bontrager\, Sarah Lea
 ffer\, and Owen Walters.\n\n\n\n"The cast has is a wonderful blend of seaso
 ned actors and relatively new actors\, but all of them have blended togethe
 r to create an ensemble that is strong in leadership\, personality\, and ta
 lent. I am very proud of the professional way in which they have approached
  this heavy piece\, and the actors have worked hard to realize these charac
 ters both as classical literary figures and as modern personalities with mo
 dern challenges\," Moss says.\n\n\n\n"Balancing lighthearted anachronisms w
 ith the serious nature of the dramatic situations Ibsen's characters find t
 hemselves in is a welcomed challenge of this project. I am proud of TOP for
  taking on two well-known and courageous plays and finding the freedom to e
 xplore them in ways that this community has never seen before."\n\n\n\nQUOT
 ES FROM A DOLLS HOUSE CAST:\n"It is very exciting to work on a piece that i
 s still socially relevant today\," says IU freshman Meg Duell\, who portray
 s Nora. "Although women have come a long way since Ibsen's time\, the play'
 s themes and messages translate to a new audience that face different obsta
 cles."\n\n\n\n"There is a continuing theme of manipulation throughout the s
 how that provides political commentary on how citizens in 21st Century view
  each other: as playthings\," says freshman Nicholas Maudlin\, who portrays
  Torvald. "I hope audiences can enjoy the subtleties behind the dialogue an
 d enjoy the humor of the show as a whole."\n\n\n\n"I hope audiences realize
  that the characters portrayed on stage are more than they seem because nob
 ody is universally villainous or universally heroic\," says IU junior Eric 
 Van Gucht\, who portrays Dr. Rank. "I hope audiences leave the show questio
 ning the characters' motives but also realizing how relevant Ibsen's argume
 nts are today."\n\n\n\nQUOTES FROM AN ENEMY OF THE PEOPLE CAST:\n"I hope au
 diences will accept our new and interpretation of the script. I think the a
 bsurdist point of view we are taking is rather enlightening in a way that m
 eans we can take risks and have the audience will learn from those risks\,"
  said IU freshman and Assistant Director Sean Cole. "Think of it as an out 
 of body experience—just sit back and enjoy what you are experiencing."\n\n\
 n\n"This is NOT classic Ibsen. I am NOT the classical Thomas Stockmann. We 
 are NOT your average production team\," says Bloomington High School South 
 senior Erica Cunningham\, who portrays Dr. Stockmann. "I hope audiences can
  experience a new spin on classic theatre and a fresh new kind of theatre a
 ll together."
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051309Z
LOCATION:Waldron Arts Center
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Project I: The Henrik Ibsen Revival
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
