UID:c3qktqpdslhj3fdtburg24uh5c@google.com
CREATED:20090721T132851Z
DESCRIPTION:“Delete”\nby: Jeremy Kennedy\n\n+\n\n“Oneliners”:\nA group show
  of drawings\, organized by Joseph Rohrer + Anne Roecklein and including an
  engaging variety local and national artists\n\nFriday July 24th\n@ Hospita
 l (102 e. Allen)\n8-10 pm\n\nAfter over a decade of living and working in B
 loomington\, Indiana\, Jeremy Kennedy’s days as a resident are numbered. An
 d\, as things come to end in Bloomington\, they will begin anew elsewhere. 
 This is\, in part\, the driving idea behind this new performance piece.\n\n
 Essentially\, the performance of “Delete” goes as follows:\n\nKennedy will 
 set up a desk and his desktop computer in the gallery space. The monitor sc
 reen will be projected on a wall in the gallery\, so that everyone can see 
 as he deletes all files\, one by one.\n\nThe hard drive in question has bee
 n the artist’s main storage unit for data including audio/video recordings 
 from the fmsmprc record label (which he helps maintain)\, photos of friends
  and previous art work\, and other important documents.\n\n“Delete” differs
  greatly from Kennedy’s previous work in the sense that this is reflective 
 of events unfolding in his personal life. As an artist and sound maker he h
 as never really allowed personal feelings and views to enter into his work.
  It’s an out with the old type of thing. A way of thinking that is relative
 ly new to this stage of Kennedy’s life\, as he is leaving these Midwestern 
 hills for the shores of sunny California.\n\nThere is also a certain signif
 icance to his choosing a computer as the vehicle for expression. Lines can 
 be drawn to such concepts as the constant movement and turnaround of techno
 logical advancement. Seen here as Kennedy’s Sony Vaio\, hailed as a remarka
 ble piece of equipment when purchased several years ago\, is soon to be a r
 elic of that era of PC age. Or perhaps\, entertaining the idea that files o
 n one’s personal hard drive have become valuable property. An idea which is
  quickly defeated in moments of dissonant human clarity\, which tells some 
 of us\, “It’s nothing but a bunch of 1’s and 0’s...”\, and doesn’t really m
 atter.\n\nAll in all\, this show could be titled “Purging”. The years spent
  here in Bloomington have left the artist as the sole owner of a great numb
 er of possessions. Some of which have great personal value\, and some of wh
 ich can be classified as “junk”\, depending on the point of view. Regardles
 s\, it’s time to downsize.\n-tyco riley (2009)\n\n---\n\nThe group drawing 
 show “Oneliners” will be presented at Art Hospital in Bloomington\, Indiana
  on Friday July 24th in concert with the “Delete” performance by artist Jer
 emy Kennedy. The show teems with drawings that deal with the title “Oneline
 rs” in some way\, shape\, or form- often in as quick and witty responses to
  multiple meanings of the show’s title. An engaging variety of both local a
 nd national artists are included.
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T051320Z
LOCATION:Art Hospital
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Art Opening: "delete" + "oneliners"
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
