DESCRIPTION:401 N. Morton Street<Br><Br>\n\nBloomington has been named the 
 nation’s 27th Certified Community Wildlife Habitat. Zionsville is the only 
 other Indiana community to have earned the certification.\n<br><Br>\nWe wil
 l celebrate at City Hall - Finger Food provided by Food Works for Middle Wa
 y House will be set up outside the council chambers from 11 to 1pm and will
  be available to the people of the city. The Mayor will accept the certific
 ate at 11pm from the National Wildlife Federation representative.\n<Br><Br>
 \nLocal wildlife advocates enrolled in the program in January 2002\, aiming
  to earn enough points for community certification. Each residential yard t
 hat meets the standards and registers as a Certified Wildlife Habitat earns
  the community one point\; points also can be earned for each place of wors
 hip\, school\, community park and business that qualifies and registers. A 
 community the size of Bloomington must earn 300 points\, according to the f
 ederation\, which keeps tabs on community progress.\n<Br><br>\nFive local s
 chools plus a site at Indiana University have qualified as Certified Wildli
 fe Habitats\, as have two places of worship — the Friends Meeting House and
  the Unitarian Univeralist Church\; seven community parks and gardens — inc
 luding the Banneker Center and Crestmont Community Gardens and Bryan Park —
  and 241 residential yards.\n<br><Br>\nBloomington Parks and Recreation Dep
 artment and the Center for Sustainable Living spearheaded the community des
 ignation and provided training for local habitat stewards.\n
