DESCRIPTION:The Bishop presents: \n\nJOE PUG & THE HUNDRED MILE BAND \n(you
  may have seen him opening for Steve Earl at The Buskirk) \nVANDAVEER \n(fr
 om Washington\, DC) \nNATE JACKSON \n(Bloomington) \n@ The Bishop \nTuesday
 \, Oct 12 \n8pm \n$8 adv/ $10 day of show \n18+ \n\nJOE PUG & THE HUNDRED M
 ILE BAND\nhttp://www.myspace.com/thejoepug \nA singer/songwriter whose song
 s offer an updated version of the country-folk archetypes that Bob Dylan an
 d John Prine made famous\, Joe Pug was born and raised in Maryland\, and be
 gan playing guitar when he was in grade school. After graduating from high 
 school\, he traveled south to North Carolina to attend college. Pug was stu
 dying writing plays\, but in time his growing passion for songwriting overw
 helmed his interest in drama. Pug dropped out of school and relocated to Ch
 icago\, where he supported himself as a carpenter while he wrote songs and 
 began working his way into the city's club circuit. He released his debut E
 P\, 'Nation of Heat'\, on his own label in 2008. The EP garnered attention 
 from established musicians such as M. Ward and Josh Ritter\, with whom Pug 
 toured\, as well as invitations to play at both Lollapalooza and Newport Fo
 lk Festival. No stranger to Bloomington\, Pug opened for Steve Earle at The
  Buskirk-Chumley Theatre last year. After touring extensively\, Pug release
 d his first full-length record\, 'Messenger'\, on Lightning Rod in February
  of this year. On this tour\, Joe is performing with his Hundred Mile Band 
 featuring multi-instrumentalist Bucky Baxter (who can be seen performing wi
 th Bob Dylan's band\, and has appeared on recordings by Bob Dylan\, R.E.M.\
 , Steve Earl\, Ryan Adams\, and more) and bassist Matt Schuessler. \n\n“Whi
 le most singer/songwriters are content to warble out a few semi-clever turn
 s of phrase\, Pug’s scorching poetry and soulful\, ‘every phrase\ncould be 
 my last’ voice will stop you cold. If you want to read the actual endorseme
 nt\, touch the Braille stretching up my arms. Twenty years\nfrom now\, lazy
  journalists will compare every halfway decent songwriter to Joe Pug. Mark 
 my words.” – Jason Killingsworth\, Paste Magazine\n\n“One of the most surpr
 ising facts about singer-songwriter Joe Pug is that he’s only 23 years old.
  Not only does Chicago-based singer have a beautifully mature voice\, but t
 he lyrics on Pug’s debut EP\, Nation of Heat\, possess wisdom seemingly bey
 ond his age.” – NPR \n\n\nVANDAVEER \nhttp://www.vandaveer.net/ \nhttp://ww
 w.myspace.com/vandaveer \nVANDAVEER is the alt-folk song-singing/record mak
 ing/globetrotting project penned and put forth by DC-by-way-of-Kentucky tun
 esmith Mark Charles Heidinger.\nVandaveer’s debut album\, Grace & Speed\, a
  mostly live\, stripped down affair\, swiftly entered this great big dusty 
 world in the spring of 2007. The press responded heartily\, with The Washin
 gton Post saying Vandaveer “revives the earnestness of the pre-psychedelic 
 60’s\,” and XM Cafe calling him “this generation’s Nick Drake.” \n\n"The mu
 sic on Vandaveer’s second album\, Divide & Conquer\, reflects the duality a
 n old soul and a little child trapped in a 32-year-old’s body\, with lyrics
  referencing both World War I and children’s author Beverly Cleary in daydr
 eamy songs featuring rich piano melodies and the pure\, delicate voice of b
 andmate Rose Guerin." – PASTE\n\n\nNATE JACKSON \nhttp://www.myspace.com/np
 jackson \nBloomingtopian Americana. \n\n\n\n\n\nhttp://www.thebishopbar.com
  \nhttp://www.myspace.com/thebishopbar \n\n\n\nhttp://www.facebook.com/even
 t.php?eid=157664884258804
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T052122Z
LOCATION:The Bishop \, 123 S. Walnut St. \, Bloomington\, IN
SEQUENCE:256553
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:JOE PUG (opened for Steve Earle at the Buskirk)\, VANDAVEER @ The B
 ishop 
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
