CREATED:20090330T203637Z
DESCRIPTION:Bloomington - Indiana University’s African American Arts Instit
 ute Choral Ensemble will host its’ Annual Spring Concert on Saturday\, Apri
 l 11\, 8:00 p.m. at the John Waldron Arts Center\, located on 122 S. Walnut
  Street\, Bloomington\, IN.  \n\nThis year’s theme is “From Africa to Ellin
 gton”.  The concert will feature traditional folk songs of Ghana paralleled
  with a contemporary spiritual arrangement of traditional favorites.  Also 
 featured\, is a piece entitled “Psalm Shout” by 1968 Jacobs School of Music
  graduate\, Dr. Robert Morris.  This song infuses elements of Stravinsky’s 
 Symphony of Psalms with Ragtime and Gospel making it suitable for the conce
 rt stage and the “praise house”! \n\nThe contemporary groups\, God’s Progre
 ss\, Sojourner and Soul Ace will also be featured.  The groups come out of 
 the African American Choral Ensemble whose music resonates with the complex
 ities of the black American experience.  The three contemporary gospel grou
 ps honor and preserve the vocal traditions of black gospel music.  They hav
 e opened for such legendary performers as The Five Blind Boys of Alabama an
 d Ray Charles.\nThe Gospel music will feature music from recordings of grea
 t notable artist such as Sam Cooke\, “Nearer My God to Thee”\, and contempo
 rary hits by John P. Kee\, Richard Smallwood\, The Florida Mass Choir and o
 thers.  Original student works by Alumni John Harris and Calvin Carrington 
 will also be highlighted.\n\nThe concert will conclude with a tribute to th
 e great Duke Ellington featuring works from his sacred concerts performed b
 etween 1965 and 1968\, complete with Big Band\, IU Jazz Band\, under the di
 rection of Jeremy Allen\, Chorus and  tap dancer Vanessa Owens\, Artistic D
 irector of Kenyette’ Dance Company\, Indianapolis.  Although Ellington wrot
 e quite extensive orchestration of the Big Band\, many of the choral number
 s were in unison.  This led the African American Choral Ensemble director\,
  Keith McCutchen\, to arrange several of the works\, namely\, “Come Sunday”
 \, “Hallelujah”\, and “David Danced”\, elaborating on choral participation 
 and a mixture of Big Band writing that’s injected with gospel elements.  \n
 \nGeneral Admission: $17 for Adults and $7 for Students and Children (limit
  2 per student I.D.)\n\nContact Information:\nOlivia Hairston\nAssistant to
  the Performance Manager\nAfrican American Arts Institute\n812.855.5427\nol
 ihairs@indiana.edu  \nwww.indiana.edu/~aaai\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20230221T052219Z
LOCATION:The Waldron Auditorium
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:African American Choral Ensemble Spring Concert
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
